-- title:
--   Lemma 7.2 — an explicit succinct discount factor approximates limit-average values
-- statement:
--   Let $G$ be a game and $r$ a reward function with values in $[0,1]$ such that all transition probabilities and rewards are integer multiples of $1/Q$ for a positive integer $Q\le2^{B_Q}$. Let $n=|S|$, $m=\max(|A|,|B|)$, $\varepsilon=2^{-B_\varepsilon}$, and
--   $$B_\lambda=\Big\lceil\log\big(4nm^n(\lceil\log n\rceil+n\lceil\log m\rceil+B_Q+3)+B_\varepsilon nm^n\big)\Big\rceil$$
--   (logarithms base 2). Then for every discount factor $\lambda\in[1-2^{-2^{B_\lambda}},1)$ and every state $s$,
--   $$|\mathrm{val}_\lambda(s)-\mathrm{val}(s)|\le\varepsilon,$$
--   where $\mathrm{val}_\lambda$ is the value of $\mathrm{Disc}(r,\lambda)$ and $\mathrm{val}$ the value of $\mathrm{LimAvg}(r)$.
--
--   This makes the limit-average value approximable by discounted values with a succinct discount factor whose exponent $B_\lambda$ is polynomial in the input size.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - encoding: a finite game G = (S, A, B, δ) with |S| = n and at most m actions per player → states Fin n, actions Fin mA and Fin mB with n, mA, mB ≥ 1, m = max(mA, mB), distributions as nonnegative real vectors summing to one; reason: relabelling of finite sets; nonempty state and action sets are the source game convention
--   - encoding: the probability measure on valid plays Ω_s generated by finite histories, strategies on valid histories → the Ionescu-Tulcea trajectory measure on all sequences in (S × A × B)^ℕ; strategies are defined on all finite stage lists; reason: invalid plays have probability zero and values of strategies on invalid histories never matter, so every expectation and value is unchanged
--   - encoding: ⌈log x⌉ (base 2) → Nat.clog 2 x; reason: equal for every positive integer x; all logarithm arguments are positive because the state and action sets are nonempty
--   - generalization: the computational accuracy convention ε = 2^{-Bε} ∈ (0,1), Bε ≥ 1 → Bε ranges over all natural numbers, also allowing ε = 1; reason: all source instances remain included; at Bε = 0 both values lie in [0,1], so their absolute difference is at most 1
-- source:
--   Ali Asadi, Krishnendu Chatterjee, The Computational Complexity of Concurrent Stochastic Games: Exact Value and Approximation, STOC 2027 submission #285 (October 2026), SHA-256 5cff187cf6b230b3bdfe540f2832f824d1ddb6acb43239fbf469d37c43b0d461, https://github.com/sigact-stoc/stoc2027/releases/download/papers-1/285.pdf, Lemma 7.2 (from [OB21, Theorem 3]), printed p. 31 (PDF p. 33)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_ConcurrentStochasticApprox_Approx_Game
open Finset

namespace ConcurrentStochasticApprox.Approx

/-- **Lemma 7.2.** Let `G` be a game and `r` a reward function such that all transition
probabilities and rewards are integer multiples of `1/Q` for a positive integer `Q ≤ 2^{B_Q}`. Let
`ε = 2^{-Bε}` and `Bλ = ⌈log(4 n m^n (⌈log n⌉ + n⌈log m⌉ + B_Q + 3) + Bε n m^n)⌉`. Then for every
discount factor `λ ∈ [1 - 2^{-2^{Bλ}}, 1)` and every state `s`, `|val_λ(s) - val(s)| ≤ ε`. -/
theorem lemma_7_2 {n mA mB : ℕ} (G : CSG n mA mB) (r : Reward n mA mB) (hr : IsReward r)
    (Q BQ : ℕ) (hQ : 0 < Q) (hQB : Q ≤ 2 ^ BQ) (hden : HasCommonDenom G r Q) (Bε : ℕ) :
    ∀ lam : ℝ,
      succinct (Nat.clog 2 (4 * n * (max mA mB) ^ n *
          (Nat.clog 2 n + n * Nat.clog 2 (max mA mB) + BQ + 3) + Bε * n * (max mA mB) ^ n)) ≤ lam →
      lam < 1 → ∀ s, |discValue G r lam s - limAvgValue G r s| ≤ (1 / 2 : ℝ) ^ Bε := by sorry

end ConcurrentStochasticApprox.Approx
